00:00:00.585 --> 00:00:02.335
YUFENG GUO: Previously
on "AI Adventures,"

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:05.160
we looked at the Tensor
Processing Unit v1--

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.860
how it came to be
and its design.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.490
It only seems right that
we now go back and check

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:13.530
out TPU v2 and v3.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.440
What changes and improvements
were made to create these new

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.950
state-of-the-art chips?

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.910
And how can you use them to
power your machine learning?

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.376
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:23.376 --> 00:00:24.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.980
Welcome to "AI Adventures" where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.780
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.000
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.340
And on this episode,
we're going to take a look

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.480
at the architecture of the
TPU v2 and v3, as well as

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.150
how the hardware design
choices come together

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.390
to create a powerful,
specialized, machine learning

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.620
chip.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.870
After the TPU v1 was
created, the team

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.850
took the lessons they
learned and applied it

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.220
to designing the TPU v2.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.740
As you can see, it's
considerably larger

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:04.800
than the first TPU and features
four chips instead of just one.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.150
Additionally, it's got
some massive heat sinks.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:11.210
It has 180 teraflops
of compute, meaning

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.810
that it can do 180 trillion
floating-point operations

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.220
per second.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.700
And it does both training
and prediction now.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.940
The layout of the TPU
v2 is quite interesting.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:28.280
Each board has those four chips,
but each chip has two cores.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.700
Each core then contains a
matrix unit, a vector unit,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.150
and a scalar unit, all
connected to 8 gigabytes

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.490
of high-bandwidth memory.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:39.590
That means in total,
each board has

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.740
8 cores and 64 gigs of memory.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:49.510
And the matrix unit is a
128 x 128 systolic array.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:52.360
What's special about
the TPU v2, though,

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:57.670
is its use of a new data
type called the bfloat16.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.960
The bfloat16 combines the range
of a 32-bit floating point

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.170
number with just the
storage space of only

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.480
a 16-bit floating point number.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.750
And what does the B
in bfloat16 stand for?

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.380
Brain, since it comes to us
from the Google Brain team.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.480
Of course, you can't get
something for nothing.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.290
And what we lose is precision.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:22.540
The bfloat16 is
able to represent

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:26.500
far fewer decimal places than a
standard 16-bit floating point

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:27.440
number.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.350
However, the team
determined that this

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.690
was worth the trade-off
since neural networks

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.880
can operate at a
reduced precision

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.640
while retaining
sufficiently high accuracy.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.860
And using the bfloat16
enabled the TPU v2

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:43.060
to fit in much more
computational power.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.040
Combined with designing
an integrated chip

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.850
and placing it on the board
with three compatriots,

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.920
so you have four
chips per board,

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.830
the TPU v2 achieves much
higher performance than the v1.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.920
Now for the really
exciting part.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.010
The TPU v2 is
arranged into pods.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.760
One TPU v2 pod is 64 TPUs.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.330
They're all connected together,
and you can use an entire pod

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:13.820
as if it was one machine.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.440
And since there are
two cores per chip

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.210
with those four chips per
board and 64 TPUs per pod,

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:25.100
that multiplies out to
512 cores in a TPU pod,

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:30.890
totaling up to over 11
petaflops of processing power.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:33.410
You can use smaller
subdivisions of the pods

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:37.520
as well, such as, say, a
quarter pod or a half pod.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.760
What's really extraordinary is
when you start using TPU pods

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.970
to train up state-of-the-art
models on benchmark data sets.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.470
For example, you can
train ResNet-50 in 30--

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.500
not hours, but 30 minutes.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.970
And that's only on a half
pod with just 32 TPUs.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.500
And when you look at the
training process used,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.410
they're using a batch size
of 8,000 images per batch.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:04.520
Quite amazing.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:06.860
And this is part of what
allows that training to proceed

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.620
at such a breakneck pace.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.300
Now I promised to talk
about TPU v3, as well.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.580
So here it is.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:17.209
As you can see, they took
the v2 and they made it blue.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:20.209
But additionally, of course,
these chips use water cooling.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:22.940
This allows it to take up
much less vertical space.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:28.990
So the TPU v3 pods can then
support many more TPUs in it.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:33.290
And a full TPU v3 pod comes
in at eight times faster

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:38.180
than a v2 pod and weighs
in at over 100 petaflops

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.990
of compute power.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.920
Today, TPUs are available
as individual boards

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.780
or as pod slices.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:50.460
And both TPU v2 and TPU v3
are available for you to use.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.660
It only costs a couple
of dollars per hour,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.920
depending on which region you
use and which TPU you choose.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.250
Pricing for TPU pods is,
as you might imagine,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:02.040
somewhat more substantial
with all those TPUs connected

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:02.980
together.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.180
But for truly
large-scale training jobs

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.491
that you need to have run
quickly, it is worth it.

00:05:10.491 --> 00:05:13.080
Programming the TPU
is just consistently

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.760
getting easier and easier.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.070
With the recent release
of TensorFlow 2.0,

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:20.190
you can even use the
Keras API to code up

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.110
your training for TPUs.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.110
There are great samples
and implementations of many

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.610
state-of-the-art models.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:30.660
So what you can do is just
swap in your custom data set,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.100
and you're off to the races.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:37.260
You can learn more and get
started at g.co/cloudtpu.

